  "body": "When volume is set to `0` and the `muted` property transitions from `true` to `false` (unmutes) it seems most helpful to then set the `volume` of the element to something higher than `0`.\r\n\r\nCurrently the default Chrome and Safari control bars both allow toggling mute by clicking the volume icon. However if volume is set to `0` this toggling has no effect (that the user can perceive). Firefox on the other hand using the built in control bar functions more as I would expect. If the volume is `0` when you click the toggle to unmute the element it sets the volume to `0.5`\r\n\r\nI can see a few reasons why this may not be appropriate.\r\n\r\n1) Probably the biggest is that there could be applications out there that rely on setting `volume` to `0` then `muted` to `false` currently (to implement an audio fade in or some such) and this would break that behavior.\r\n\r\n1) This effect is already achievable with custom control bars\r\n\r\nWhat are peoples thoughts?",
